    {
      "id": "competing-calls",
      "text": "Start with an unused Once and two overlapping Do calls with distinct callbacks. If the earlier entrant pauses before acquiring the mutex, the later entrant can acquire it first and run its callback. Either caller that reads done as false takes the mutex path and skips its callback if the winner has set done by the time it acquires the lock. A caller that reads done as true skips the mutex and can return after the winning callback finishes even while the winner's Do call is still unwinding.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 74,
        "fragment": "o.m.Lock()"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the documentation's “first call” wording specify that the winner of overlapping calls is the caller that acquires the mutex first?"
    },
    {
      "id": "callback-completion",
      "text": "Start with an unused Once and a callback that writes shared state in a defer, launches a goroutine, and returns normally. The callback's defer runs before Do stores done, so a competing Do call can rely on the deferred write after returning. If the launched goroutine has not finished when the callback returns, a competing Do call can return while that goroutine is still running.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 77,
        "fragment": "defer o.done.Store(true)"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "panic-consumes-once",
      "text": "Start with an unused Once. Call Do with a callback that panics and lets the panic escape, or pass a nil callback so the attempted call itself panics. During unwinding, Do stores done and unlocks the mutex. The panic propagates to the initiating caller, while a waiting or later Do call returns without invoking its own callback or replaying the panic. Passing nil after the Once is done returns normally because the callback is skipped.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 50,
        "fragment": "If f panics, Do considers it to have returned"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "cancellation-and-goroutine-exit",
      "text": "Start with an unused Once. One goroutine enters its callback and another calls Do on the same instance while the callback runs. Canceling a context used by either goroutine does not itself unblock the waiter or change done. If the callback responds by returning, Do records completion and unlocks. If it calls runtime.Goexit, the deferred store and unlock still run, the initiating goroutine exits without returning from Do, and the waiting caller returns without running its callback.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 75,
        "fragment": "defer o.m.Unlock()"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the completion contract explicitly cover runtime.Goexit, since waiting calls can return even though the callback never returns to its caller?"
    },
    {
      "id": "reentrant-call",
      "text": "Start with an unused Once whose callback calls Do synchronously on that same instance before returning. The outer call holds the mutex and done is false, so the inner call waits for a lock the outer call cannot release while the callback is blocked. If the callback instead starts a goroutine that calls Do on the same instance and returns without waiting for it, the outer call can complete and the goroutine's callback is skipped whether it arrives before or after completion. A synchronous call on a separate unused Once can also complete if it does not depend on the outer call.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 48,
        "fragment": "Do to be called, it will deadlock."
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the deadlock warning specify that the nested call targets the same Once and that the outer callback depends on its completion?"
    }
